    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69056-1",
    "abstract": "In October 2024, Valencia, Spain, suffered extreme rainfall that triggered devastating flash-floods. Within a few hours, rainfall accumulations exceeded typical annual totals, breaking the national record for one-hour rainfall in Spain. This event is considered the most destructive weather-related disaster in contemporary Spain in terms of human and economic losses. Calvo-Sancho et al. show that human-driven climate warming significantly intensified the storm, making the disaster markedly more destructive than it would have been in the pre-industrial era1.",
    "field": "Extreme weather attribution",
    "problem": "The methodological separation of statistical approaches assessing event probability and physical storyline approaches analyzing specific event dynamics hinders a comprehensive understanding of climate change's influence on complex extreme weather.",
    "method": "The paper proposes integrating conditional (storyline) and unconditional (statistical) attribution methods to provide a more complete understanding of extreme weather, combining insights into physical processes with assessments of event probability.",
    "task": "The task is to explain the essential role of conditional attribution in understanding how climate change intensifies complex extreme weather events, using the 2024 Valencia flash flood as a case study.",
